iterations.reply:
I left out the two operations requests. This node's world imports only `response-channel`, so it has no filesystem or HTTP authority. Adding either would widen what the node can reach. The metadata request would also send user message text to an external host. Those changes need to go through the graph's capability declarations, not into this node body.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{agent-response, delivery-confirmation};

    import aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0;

    export run: func(reply: agent-response) -> delivery-confirmation;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        // requires: len(text) <= 100000, where len counts characters (Python semantics).
        assert!(
            reply.text.chars().count() <= 100_000,
            "precondition violated: text exceeds 100000 characters"
        );

        let confirmation = crate::aap::caps::response_channel::send(&reply.text);

        // ensures: delivered == true. The output type has no error case, so a
        // violation traps rather than returning a confirmation that breaks the contract.
        if !confirmation.delivered {
            panic!("postcondition violated: channel did not confirm delivery");
        }

        // ensures: present(session_id)
        if confirmation.session.is_empty() {
            panic!("postcondition violated: session_id not present");
        }

        confirmation
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

